  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A **weighted directed graph** consists of a set VVV of **vertices** and a collection EEE of **edges**. Each edge e∈Ee \\in Ee∈E is an ordered triple e=(x,y,w)e = (x, y, w)e=(x,y,w) where x∈Vx \\in Vx∈V and y∈Vy \\in Vy∈V are vertices, called the **start** and **end** vertices of eee, respectively, and www is a number called the **weight** of eee.\n\nA **walk** from vertex sss to vertex ttt is an alternating sequence of vertices and edges (v1,e1,v2,e2,…,ek−1,vk)(v_1, e_1, v_2, e_2, \\ldots, e_{k-1}, v_k)(v1​,e1​,v2​,e2​,…,ek−1​,vk​) (there are kkk vertices and k−1k - 1k−1 edges in this sequence) satisfying the following:\n\n\nk≥1k \\ge 1k≥1;\nv1=sv_1 = sv1​=s;\nvk=tv_k = tvk​=t;\nThe start vertex of eie_iei​ is viv_ivi​ (for each iii from 111 to k−1k - 1k−1);\nThe end vertex of eie_iei​ is vi+1v_{i+1}vi+1​ (for each iii from 111 to k−1k - 1k−1).\nYou are given a special weighted directed graph (V,E)(V, E)(V,E). The weights of the edges in this graph are **complex numbers**, that is, numbers of the form a+bia + bia+bi. Each complex weight a+bia + bia+bi satisfies the following:\n\n\naaa and bbb are integers;\na2+b2&lt;5a^2 + b^2 &lt; 5a2+b2&lt;5.\nWe say that a walk from sss to ttt is **real** if the **product** of the weights of the edges in it is a real number. We call this product the **cost** of the walk.\n\nGiven two vertices sss and ttt, is there a **real** walk from sss to ttt? If so, what is the minimum possible cost of any real walk from sss to ttt? Note that:\n\n\nIt may be possible for the costs of the real walks to be arbitrarily small; you must also detect if this is the case.\nIf aaa and bbb are integers for every edge weight a+bia + bia+bi, then it can be shown that the cost of any real walk is an integer. In particular, the minimum possible cost is an integer.\nThe minimum possible cost may be very large, so only output it modulo 998244353998244353998244353.\nThe product of two complex numbers a+bia + bia+bi and a′+b′ia' + b'ia′+b′i is defined by multiplying them via the distributive property and then reducing i2=−1i^2 = -1i2=−1. For example:\n\n(3+4i)⋅(4−7i)=3⋅4−3⋅7i+4i⋅4−4i⋅7i=12−21i+16i−28i2=12−21i+16i−28(−1)=40−5i.\\begin{align*}\n(3 + 4i)\\cdot (4 - 7i)\n&amp;= 3\\cdot 4 - 3\\cdot 7i + 4i\\cdot 4 - 4i\\cdot 7i \\\\\n&amp;= 12 - 21i + 16i - 28i^2 \\\\\n&amp;= 12 - 21i + 16i - 28(-1) \\\\\n&amp;= 40 - 5i. \n\\end{align*}(3+4i)⋅(4−7i)​=3⋅4−3⋅7i+4i⋅4−4i⋅7i=12−21i+16i−28i2=12−21i+16i−28(−1)=40−5i.​We say that a complex number a+bia + bia+bi is real if b=0b = 0b=0. In this case, we consider a+bia + bia+bi equal to the real number aaa.\n\nFor real numbers m&gt;0m &gt; 0m&gt;0 and aaa, we define (a mod m)(a \\bmod m)(amodm) as the unique real number a′a'a′ such that 0≤a′&lt;m0 \\le a' &lt; m0≤a′&lt;m and a−a′a - a'a−a′ is divisible by mmm.\n\n\nInput Format\nThe first line contains TTT, the number of test cases. The following lines describe the test cases.\n\n\nThe first line of each test case contains two space-separated integers ∣V∣|V|∣V∣ and ∣E∣|E|∣E∣, where ∣V∣|V|∣V∣ denotes the number of vertices in VVV and ∣E∣|E|∣E∣ denotes the number of edges in EEE. For simplicity, the vertices are numbered 111 to ∣V∣|V|∣V∣.\n\n\nThe second line contains two space-separated integers sss and ttt representing two vertices (which may be equal).\n\n\nThe next ∣E∣|E|∣E∣ lines describe the edges. Each of the next ∣E∣|E|∣E∣ lines contains four space-separated integers xxx, yyy, aaa and bbb denoting that (x,y,a+bi)(x, y, a + bi)(x,y,a+bi) is an edge.\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output a single line containing:\n\n\n\nAn integer denoting the minimum possible cost of any real walk from sss to ttt. (This number must be reduced modulo 998244353998244353998244353.)\nThe string `NEGATIVE INFINITY` if the costs of the real walks are arbitrarily small.\nThe string `NEGATIVE` if there is no real walk from sss to ttt.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1041 \\leq T \\leq 10^41≤T≤104",
